canon_reply: **The compressor is the biggest energy‑consumer in a refrigerator.**\n\n| Component | How it uses energy | Why it’s the largest draw |\n|-----------|--------------------|---------------------------|\n| **Compressor** | It’s an electric motor that compresses the refrigerant, raising its pressure and temperature. The motor’s power rating (often 200–400 W for a typical 1‑ton fridge) is the direct electrical load. | The compressor does the work that moves heat from inside the fridge to the outside. All other parts (condenser, expansion valve, evaporator) only “pass” the refrigerant; they don’t consume electricity themselves. |\n| **Condenser** | The hot, high‑pressure gas releases heat to the room. The only energy it uses is the heat that the compressor has already supplied. | It’s a passive heat exchanger; it doesn’t draw power. |\n| **Expansion valve / capillary tube** | It simply drops pressure; no electrical power is used. | Passive component. |\n| **Evaporator** | The cold liquid absorbs heat from the fridge interior. Like the condenser, it only receives the energy that the compressor has already put into the system. | Passive heat exchanger. |\n\n### Bottom line\n- **Compressor**: ~70–90 % of the refrigerator’s total electrical consumption.  \n- **Other parts**: consume negligible direct power; they only affect how efficiently the compressor can do its job (e.g., a dirty condenser can make the compressor work harder).\n\nSo, if you’re looking to cut energy use, focus on keeping the compressor and its associated heat‑exchange surfaces clean and well‑insulated.
